snippet.textDisplay:
@SynFrag That's bs. Lowering the groundwater-level is not "magnitude smaller than large societal issues" - it is one cause for societal issues! Or at least it will be once it reaches a certain point. If you have less groundwater, it affects the plants. Most importantly trees, which don't use much of the surface-water but rely on the water that's saved in the soil. Not even the groundwater but the water that sits in between the surface and the groundwater. It might not be obvious to the average person but already trees show signs of water-stress. I'm less an expert on the US-trees but I bet it's not so different from our trees. Trees might not be "important", though, unless you're a farmer for fruits or almonds or nuts, so moving on to the fact that you get your drinking-water from groundwater. Once the water-level drops, pumping up more gw for consumption will cost more. Guess who has to pay for that. Water - drinkable water, that is - is a limited commodity, no matter how closed the system is. Once it drifts up into clouds and rains into a river or the ocean, it isn't usable for drinking anymore. Or not without significant cleaning-input. 

Always and always have corporations and also politicians claimed that the economical issues are more important that environmental ones, and have laughed at those who messaged about careful usage of resources. And then suddenly, the rivers are so low on water that the ships cannot pass with cargo (or with very much less cargo that usual) and things get hellishly more expensive, and nuclear power plants need to shut down because their rivers are so low that the cooling can't happen (because the water is too warm to cool anything) and suddenly, nature does get interesting. 

I get that you're saying that politically, the economical and societal issues get more pull, but don't just count out the environmental impact. It isn't feamongering - or at least, it isn't baseless fearmongering. It actually does matter, and it can happen much quicker than you might think. (not that it hasn't been talked about for ages already, but nobody listens until it hurts personally)

