snippet.textDisplay:
@PR2k9 I've got a weird split view of this whole thing. On one hand, it feels like we're speeding towards driverless cars a little too fast, and there's a lot to unpack about how Tesla's handled that rollout. On the other - I nearly died a few years back because a driver didn't look left, and pulled out in front of me while I was on a motorcycle, going 55-60 Mph. I put a 12-inch intrusion into the car(read, I buckled the driver's side door in a foot), broke both arms, and a few other things. It wasn't fun. 

Now, no amount of technology on my vehicle would have saved me. There wasn't even time to squeeze the brakes, BUT I feel like an automated system on HIS car would have seen me. A fast-moving object is well within the basic functionality of a driverless car to notice and stop to process, where the human in question just wasn't paying attention. 

Anecdotes aren't nearly as useful as real safety statistics, but this is why I'm on the fence. It's hard not to feel like I'd have fewer scars in a world with more driverless cars :)

